  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 241 AM EDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nPattern / Forecast Synopsis: \n\nStrong corridor of isentropic lift being drawn into the region at \nthis late hour as northern Michigan finds itself sandwiched between \na mature 993mb surface low in Manitoba and departing strong 1026mb \nsurface high currently making a run at the St. Lawrence River Valley \n/ northern New England. Result is the complete overwhelming of the \ndry layer from Monday as deep moisture is drawn into the region amid \nan active 40kt low level jet nosing into the region, with copious \nlift / f-gen aloft providing more than enough support for rain \nshowers to continue through the morning hours across the region once \nthe associated surface warm front passes through... which will in \nturn lead to quite the humid afternoon across the region. In \naddition, pressure gradient most pinched overhead this morning will \nlikely lead to quite a wind-driven rain for the most part... with \nsome gusts pushing 40mph on the lakeshores and the highest of \ninterior terrain, generally 25-35mph elsewhere. Once the warm front \nclears, winds should be on a downward trend as the pressure gradient \nslackens, but will still be a touch breezy through sunset.\n\nDecaying precipitation shield looks to really keep cloud cover \nintact through the day, and surface temperatures likely \nstruggle to break 70 across the Au Sable River Valley where \ncurrent trends indicate that clouds / showers should linger \nlongest. Elsewhere, temps spike into the 73-77 range as peeks of\nsun filter through and the warm nose moves firmly overhead, and\ndewpoints spike well into the 60s across the board. As such, \nthis may be just enough for residual shower / thunder development\nin the afternoon as the surface cold front passes through \nnorthern Michigan. These should be on a localized scale and \nquite transient when considering the flow, but with the zenith \nof moisture concentration overhead, an isolated downpour is \npossible... primarily favoring spots along and NORTH of a \nCadillac to Alpena line. Current trends do not support enough \ninstability to drum up additional shower activity in eastern \nupper this afternoon.\n\nThe cold front will surge through in the evening, flipping winds \nmore northwest, and commencing the intrusion of cooler and drier air \nas surface high pressure builds by Wednesday. Surface cold front \nassociated with the ongoing system looks to turn stationary over the \nlower Great Lakes west into the Corn Belt (just north of an \nimpressive late summer thermal ridge over the southeast CONUS), \nwhich should serve as the focus for additional low level jet driven \nrain / storms through midweek, likely keeping us dry through the \nrest of the week (aside from an isolated shower chance along / south \nof M-55 tonight, but guidance is trending quite bearish to this \nidea... front should be way too far south to support this anyways). \nAs such, tentative thoughts at this juncture are that we hold dry \nand seasonable through Friday with highs in the 65-75 range and lows \nin the 40-55 range. \n\nLooking at next weekend, long term guidance is warming up to the \nidea of another potent Pacific borne, southern stream wave ejecting \ninto the Plains... coupled with another deep surge in moisture \nto boot. Still a bit early to make the call, but this pattern \nhas basically started holding up a sign that says \"HEAVY RAIN: \nPASS THROUGH HERE\" with a giant, neon green arrow pointing at \nthe Corn Belt into the entirety of the Great Lakes. Will have to\nsee how amplified this wave is, as a far enough northward surge\nin moisture could certainly put us in play for another round of\nefficient to even heavy rainfall this upcoming weekend.",
